("Timur",1336,"1336–1405","Conqueror","Central Asia","Timurid Empire","Timur ('the Lame') conquered from Anatolia to Delhi, building towers of skulls; his campaigns killed perhaps 17 million. He made Samarkand magnificent and was an ancestor of Babur.","Timur","T","Timurid Empire; Timur sacks Delhi; Babur; Tughlaq dynasty; Islam"),
("Aurangzeb",1618,"1618–1707","Mughal emperor","India","Largest Mughal extent","Aurangzeb (r. 1658–1707) imprisoned his father Shah Jahan, killed his brothers, and expanded the empire to its greatest size, but reimposed the jizya, executed Guru Tegh Bahadur and spent 25 years fighting the Marathas.","Aurangzeb","R","Mughal Empire; Mughal dynasty; Shah Jahan; Chhatrapati Shivaji; Death of Aurangzeb; Sikhism; Islam"),
("Humayun",1508,"1508–1556","Mughal emperor","India","Lost and regained the empire","Babur's son Humayun lost his empire to Sher Shah Suri in 1540, spent 15 years in exile in Persia, and regained Delhi in 1555, dying after falling down library stairs.","Humayun","R","Mughal Empire; Babur; Akbar; Suri dynasty"),
("Sher Shah Suri",1486,"1486–1545","Sultan","India","Rupee and Grand Trunk Road","Sher Shah, an Afghan, defeated Humayun and ruled north India (1540–45), introducing the rupiya coin, land surveys and road networks.","Sher_Shah_Suri","R","Suri dynasty; Humayun; Mughal Empire; Islam"),
("Nur Jahan",1577,"1577–1645","Mughal empress","India","Power behind Jahangir's throne","Nur Jahan, wife of Jahangir, issued coins and decrees in her name and was the most powerful woman in Mughal history.","Nur_Jahan","R","Mughal Empire; Mughal dynasty; Islam"),
("Prithviraj Chauhan",1166,"1166–1192","King","Ajmer and Delhi","Battles of Tarain","Prithviraj III of the Chauhan dynasty defeated Muhammad of Ghor in 1191 but lost and was killed in 1192.","Prithviraj_Chauhan","R","Second Battle of Tarain; Delhi Sultanate; Hinduism"),
